<p>President Trump departed Washington for Beijing ahead of a May 13-15 state visit with Chinese President Xi Jinping, the first visit to China by a sitting U.S. president in nearly nine years. The agenda includes trade, tariffs, rare earth exports, Taiwan, artificial intelligence and the war involving the United States, Israel and Iran. Trump told reporters before leaving that he expected a “long talk” with Xi about Iran but did not need China’s help to end the war. China remains a major buyer of Iranian oil, and Washington has pressed Beijing to use its leverage with Tehran. Executives from technology, finance, aerospace and agriculture joined the presidential delegation.</p><img src='https://kagiproxy.com/img/fJ2jfs-fM5zRCbAVRBwsimzm1NNDWonl2P13_ulK2IP4o3OUzY-7T3JSjYndynjxD0AnwiryZB8NdPLqPTkIGbsoP9LCVqE7W7_kr5iK7GFRI06AJhxzT7ZPHX68fuunV_Kea8plbYsa_5K-eEVVV4z5NyBafifTziJDF4sCsKOfvi77Ut-qDjgMNtGIFpzwbwWfyGzweybs5FC7hRlcmbwCtN7LOAZUcA-dWzDnQ8eycIkWg360ld3KxgQZsB4ud5KtgvNyhh5Gluujmw' alt='People walk outside the closed Hall of Prayer for Good Harvests at the Temple of Heaven on May 12, 2026, before President Trump’s visit to Beijing.' /><br /><h3>Highlights:</h3><ul><li>Market access: Trump wrote that he would ask Xi to “open up” China to American companies. U.S. executives in the delegation are from technology, finance, aerospace and agriculture.</li><li>Taiwan issue: Trump’s plan to discuss Taiwan arms sales with Xi concerned Asian allies because negotiations with Beijing on weapons exports to Taipei would break long U.S. precedent.</li><li>Domestic pressure: Reports said Trump is seeking a visible win as the Iran war and conflict-linked inflation have weighed on his approval.</li><li>Delegation makeup: Defense Secretary Pete Hegseth joined the trip, an unusual addition for a U.S. defense chief on a presidential state visit to China, while first lady Melania Trump did not travel.</li></ul><blockquote>I don&#x27;t think we need any help with Iran. We&#x27;ll win it 